    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_03d7477d87643257c37191ed5099d35a6b6afd6800ea5d9cddc45fd8006d7fdd",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Li's sequence changes the representation of RH from a location condition on every nontrivial zero to sign tests on scalar coefficients. The logarithmic-derivative description reads each coefficient from completed-function data near a distinguished point, while the symmetrically interpreted sum makes it an aggregate of the global zero set. This compression gains a countable positivity interface but loses the location of any individual zero: the equivalence requires every coefficient, and the symmetric convergence prescription is part of the criterion rather than dispensable notation.",
